body:
ok, I tried to follow the code to get more info on this.

`get_theme_owner_node()` returns either a window, a control, or nullptr. if it's a window, because of the conversion it would become nullptr, so that means `Control` or `nullptr`.

the `Control` in `ThemeOwner` is assigned with `set_owner_node()`.
this is **only** called from window or control with `set_theme_owner_node()`.
`set_theme_owner_node()` is called **only** in `propagate_theme_changed()`, which tests if the theme is valid when assigning:

```c++
		if (c != p_owner_node && c->get_theme().is_valid()) {
			// Has a theme, so we don't want to change the theme owner,
			// but we still want to propagate in case this child has theme items
			// it inherits from the theme this node uses.
			// See https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/62844.
			assign = false;
		}

		if (assign) {
			c->set_theme_owner_node(p_owner_node);
		}
```

`is_valid()` is an inline function of the `Ref` class that returns `false` if the "reference" is `nullptr`.
```c++
inline bool is_valid() const { return reference != nullptr; }
```

what is returned by `get_theme()` is a `Ref<Theme>`, and `get_theme()` has a `ERR_READ_THREAD_GUARD_V(Ref<Theme>());` which I think would throw an error if it was null but I'm not sure if it would do more.

so, in short, I think I was right to thrust encapsulation to handle this, maybe there could be a situation in which the theme is somehow removed without updating `owner_control` in `theme_owner`, which is `private`, but that would probably break more than just this part of the code.

